<Project
  title="Kronos"
  subtitle="A multi-tenant WebAssembly runtime in Rust that refuses a module before it runs if the module imports file or network access its tenant was not granted."
  meta="Technical report, 2026 · with Ajax Li · Rust"
  links={[
    { label: 'Technical report (PDF)', href: '/papers/kronos.pdf', external: true },
  ]}
  description="Kronos is a multi-tenant WebAssembly runtime that checks each module's imports against its tenant's capabilities before instantiating it. Technical report by John Abrahams and Ajax Li, University of Pennsylvania."
>
  <h2>Summary</h2>
  <p>
    Kronos runs untrusted WebAssembly modules for many tenants inside one
    process, on top of Wasmtime. A module can reach the host only through the
    functions it imports, so Kronos checks that import list against the
    tenant's capabilities before instantiating the module. If the module
    imports a file or network function the tenant was not granted, it is
    refused and none of its code runs. Every invocation also gets a fuel budget
    and a wall-clock deadline.
  </p>
  <p>
    The technical report describes the design, audits every code path that
    instantiates a module, and reports measurements from two benchmark runs.
  </p>

  <h2>Design</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Import check before instantiation.</strong> File and network
      functions require <code>AllowFilesystem</code> or
      <code>AllowNetworking</code>, and WASI functions require the filesystem
      capability. The check runs before a store is created.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Metering.</strong> Wasmtime fuel bounds the instructions a guest
      executes, and an epoch counter advanced every millisecond enforces a
      wall-clock deadline.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Threaded guests.</strong> Guests compiled with
      <code>-pthread</code> run through a custom <code>wasi:thread-spawn</code>
      handler that puts workers on a shared Rayon pool and reports a worker
      trap as an error instead of exiting the host process.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What the audit found</h2>
  <p>
    The check was enforced on the server's request path. A fast instantiation
    path in the library skipped it, the threaded path granted filesystem access
    unconditionally, and the thread handler stopped workers by advancing an
    epoch counter that every tenant shares, which could expire other tenants'
    deadlines. The report covers each case and what would fix it.
  </p>

  <h2>Measurements</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Runtime</th><th>Memory per added isolate</th><th>Peak burst throughput</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Kronos</td><td>0.15&nbsp;MiB</td><td>14K req/s</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Wasmtime (Go embedding)</td><td>0.21 to 0.23&nbsp;MiB</td><td>103K to 164K req/s</td></tr>
      <tr><td>wazero</td><td>2.2&nbsp;MiB</td><td>157K to 171K req/s</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Two runs on one Apple Silicon Mac, with a small module and few trials.
    Kronos used less memory per added isolate than both Go-hosted runtimes and
    had 7 to 12 times lower burst throughput. A four-thread matrix multiply
    beat a build that called the host for every element, but a plain
    single-threaded module beat both, so the runs show no parallel speedup.
  </p>

  <h2>Status</h2>
  <p>
    This runtime is the research ancestor of
    <a href="https://kronoslabs.dev">kronoslabs.dev</a>, which now uses a
    different authority model built on WASI preopened directories. The code is
    in a private repository and is available on request; the report names the
    exact commits it describes.
  </p>

  <p class="muted">
    Co-authored with <strong>Ajax Li</strong> (UPenn). Thank you to the
    architecture group at Penn for early feedback.
  </p>
</Project>
